<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pyrenees</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pyrenees are a mountain range separating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. After conquering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> from 711 CE, Umayyad forces crossed the Pyrenees into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francia">Francia</a>. Their advance was halted at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tours">Battle of Tours</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a> in 732 CE, marking the limit of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a>'s expansion in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pyrenees.jpg" alt="Pyrenees" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Nhamblen at English Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Central_pyrenees.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The valleys of Andorra, high in the eastern <b>Pyrenees</b> between the County of Foix to the north and Catalonia to the south, appear among the parishes of the diocese of Urgell in a document traditionally dated to 839, and a later legend credited Charlemagne with granting the Andorrans…</blockquote><a href="/empire/andorra">Andorra · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Peter II had himself crowned by Pope Innocent III in Rome in 1204, fought at the Battle of Las Navas de Tolosa in 1212 and was killed at the Battle of Muret in 1213, ending the Crown’s ambitions north of the <b>Pyrenees</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-aragon">Crown of Aragon · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…the Great, who ruled from the 990s until 1030, made the dukes among the most respected princes of the kingdom, and his son Guy-Geoffrey, who reigned as William VIII, acquired the Duchy of Gascony in the 1050s and 1060s, extending ducal lordship from the Loire to the <b>Pyrenees</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-aquitaine">Duchy of Aquitaine · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The newly conquered lands of Gallaecia, Asturia and Cantabria were attached to Citerior, probably during Augustus' visit of 16–13 BCE, creating a vast province that stretched from the <b>Pyrenees</b> and the Mediterranean coast to the Atlantic shores of Galicia and south to Carthago Nova.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hispania-tarraconensis">Hispania Tarraconensis · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Aragon took its name from a river in the central <b>Pyrenees</b>, where a small county around Jaca appeared in the early ninth century under Frankish influence, ruled by counts such as Aznar Galíndez.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-aragon">Kingdom of Aragon · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>With papal backing he gathered Peter II of Aragon, Sancho VII of Navarre and crusaders from beyond the <b>Pyrenees</b>, and on 16 July 1212 destroyed the Almohad army at the Battle of Las Navas de Tolosa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-castile">Kingdom of Castile · Military</a></li></ul></section>
